---
date: 2026-06-05T16:40:21-04:00
---

Teller's Run was a neighborhood at the northernmost point of [[Geography/Calaria/Hallia/index|Hallia]]. Originally planned to be part of the city, it was separated from the neighboring [[Geography/Calaria/Hallia/Masque District/index|Masque District]] by one of the highways that pass through Hallia, and it never recovered the wealth that the separation cost it. It is cramped and dirty, its buildings built on top of one another, and filled with people who do hard labor for little money.

No subway stations run into Teller's Run, and the corporations that have replaced law enforcement in [[Geography/Calaria/Hallia/index|Hallia]] rarely go there, except when somebody from outside has made their way in and gotten into trouble. Hallia has been content to let the district manage itself. Gangs appear and die out there with some regularity, most of them lasting only as long as it takes them to shoot somebody the orgs care about.

[[Org/Pyrelight/index|Pyrelight]] is the exception. Out of Teller's Run it grew into something with a hold on the district, and from there its reach has started to cross into the Masque District.

A wall of fire was raised around the district in 862ag and has not come down. The government's attempts to reclaim Teller's Run failed, and an accord between the [[Org/Iridescent Church/index|Church]] and [[Org/Pyrelight/index|Pyrelight]] left the district to itself. Years before the fire, an aeronautics company began a skyscraper at the center of the neighborhood, meaning to move its headquarters there and renew the place; [[Org/Hildebrandt Corporation/index|Hildebrandt]] pushed it out of the market and the tower was left unfinished.

The periodicals tell what became of the tower. Six months after the fire closed the district, lights showed inside it as if it were being lived in. A few months later an emissary came out through the cordon to say that people would be admitted to a party held inside by [[Divinity/Ashen Pact/Pyre|Pyre]]. Music has been heard from the tower since, and an event is held there about every other week. The name [[Org/Embercall Industries/index|Embercall]] is first recorded in the same reports: of the rescuers who went through the flame, most never came back, and the few who did spoke of the Embercall and died soon after.

There are three known ways in and out of Teller's Run. An invitation to the parties in the district's one skyscraper admits the bearer and up to one vehicle through the fire cordon, but the invitations are written to named guests and carry a cover fee of 25,000 gold, high enough that only people who could spend that every two or three weeks are let in; the guest list is long and full of celebrities and business people, so a lesser-known guest could be impersonated. Dropping over the wall was tried once, early on, when a group of [[Org/Laity|Laity]] mercenaries parachuted in; nobody saw what became of them and the cordon still stands. The third way is underneath, through the [[Geography/Calaria/Hallia/Sewers|sewers]] or the [[Geography/Quiet Below/index|Quiet Below]], where Prime Meridian keeps no public maps and the tunnels are not mapped at all. The sewers can be entered by the public exit in [[Geography/Calaria/Hallia/Sableclutch/index|Sableclutch]], and short of the district's border they pass a sewage works.

The wall of fire exists only in [[Færrin]] and not across the horizon.

After two years behind the cordon, with [[Divinity/Ashen Pact/Pyre|Pyre]] worshipped inside, some of the district's people have turned into fire. The fire takes hold of the residents themselves, and visitors from outside the wall do not burn the same way.

An explosion in the works beneath the northern end of the district brought down what little infrastructure remained there. The blast was contained within the cordon and mostly underground, but a mushroom cloud of ash climbed over the north end and drifted south into the [[Geography/Calaria/Hallia/Masque District/index|Masque District]], where people covered their mouths against it. The district's one skyscraper was untouched, a faint sheen around it holding the falling ash away.
